Guard sentinel for tests/burst_load.sh (see its "Guard 1" block).

On 2026-10-02 an `rsync --delete` into the directory that then held these
four .sql files removed all of them. The burst driver filtered pgbench's
stderr through `grep`, so every burst for the next three hours was a no-op
that LOOKED like it worked: pgbench printed "could not open file", grep
dropped it, the driver carried on. Three hours of captures were lost.

This file is tracked in git, so it travels with the repo and with
box-check's own rsync of the repo. What it detects is therefore NOT
"somebody rsynced the repo here" (box-check does exactly that, by design --
from the repo, which contains this file) but "this directory is not an
intact checkout of tests/burst_sql": a --delete from an unrelated source,
a partial copy, or a hand-made directory passed via PGWT_BURST_SQL_DIR.

It is deliberately only the FIRST of five guards, because on its own it
cannot see a stale-but-present .sql file. The other four are in
burst_load.sh: per-file non-empty+readable, pgbench's exit status checked
directly (never through a pipe), a per-invocation achieved-tps floor, and
an independent end-to-end row-count delta on the WAL burst's target table.
